The important part:

```bash
pkill -f php-fpm
pkill: killing pid 6537 failed: Operation not permitted
```

This means **PHP-FPM is running as another user (probably root)**, so your user cannot restart/kill it. Your Symfony CLI is using that PHP-FPM process, and that is likely why the old class reference is still appearing.

Now do this:

---

### 1. Restart PHP-FPM properly

Run:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart php8.4-fpm
```

You should see no error.

Verify:

```bash
sudo systemctl status php8.4-fpm
```

It should show:

```
Active: active (running)
```

---

### 2. Restart Symfony server

Run:

```bash
symfony server:stop
```

Then:

```bash
symfony server:start
```

Keep this terminal open.

---

### 3. Trigger a fresh request

Call:

```
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/user/me
```

Then immediately:

```bash
cat var/log/dev.log
```

---

### 4. If the same error appears, let's inspect the actual runtime file

Run:

```bash
php -r "
require 'vendor/autoload.php';
\$r = new ReflectionClass('App\Security\CookieJwtAuthenticator');
echo \$r->getFileName().PHP_EOL;
"
```

Send me the output.

---

Also run:

```bash
grep -n "UserBadge" src/Security/CookieJwtAuthenticator.php
```

We expect:

```php
use Symfony\Component\Security\Http\Authenticator\Passport\Badge\UserBadge;

new UserBadge(
```

---

One more observation: your error timestamps:

```
10:58
13:13
13:18
```

are all **before** you cleared the log. So right now we don't actually have proof the error still exists after your fix.

The next fresh `dev.log` after restarting PHP-FPM will tell us the truth.
